#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "feafee48-dec9-52b5-8777-e84ab9182e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242416} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc11af3-4fbb-54df-8ae1-aed2683c10d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242417} {:answer/id #uuid "691a4e34-8391-5b62-8185-794b12b37cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242418} {:answer/id #uuid "63b01c89-8317-56c3-8ed8-0ddb40599051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242419} {:answer/id #uuid "715d6b5e-e99e-5548-bfc6-c5cddb92b53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbc11af3-4fbb-54df-8ae1-aed2683c10d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242417} :answer-field/id #uuid "c408b6a8-9a11-5dc3-8aef-976322201602" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce96ee2e-4b63-5277-ba18-2f342c520402" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-13613" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-23x+10?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $3$ does not take the above form. The value $3$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{3}{1},$ and the numerator $(3)$ is not a factor of $10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d38c6756-ab1d-5130-8cb5-b68992a13ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242423} {:answer/id #uuid "46410356-e6fc-5e4e-9752-1dfa66e94e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242424} {:answer/id #uuid "ab715e0b-1773-5856-813e-14c8b80f9a15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242425} {:answer/id #uuid "f373b34b-fdd8-5adc-83f0-075c6e252f0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242426} {:answer/id #uuid "913e0719-c51b-5767-b9ca-40fcb7ca9471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46410356-e6fc-5e4e-9752-1dfa66e94e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242424} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7b7fb63-9b3f-550d-a174-08d55f79b222" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e6af2c3-1f46-5f63-a9f5-171c18738b89" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-14142" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=6{x}^{4}-15{x}^{2}-2x+5,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{5}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 5, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{6},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\,\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083588 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98259c60-776f-5d69-b4e4-d293fe366d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080895} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd23e7f-a0b6-59fe-a380-b47116c9b174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080896} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad83be8-ec80-5b3f-9e53-07def80047c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080897} {:answer/id #uuid "7638e0c3-014d-55a8-90c1-4b5e439bf1a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080898} {:answer/id #uuid "76dd9fc6-50df-5992-be03-4ea0c5c873fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bd23e7f-a0b6-59fe-a380-b47116c9b174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080896} :answer-field/id #uuid "1009e9ba-8a5a-5de7-b4ed-6b76962873fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f572c08e-edf8-5f9d-9827-c106bc98ece4" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-39033" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x) = 3x^{3} + 5x^{2} - 15x - 25$ has a root in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$, find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-25$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-25}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 25,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{25}{3}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ are $-1$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$-\\frac{5}{3}$\n$3$\n$5$\n$-15$\n$-25$\n\n \n\n$↓$\n$-5$\n$0$\n$25$\n\n \n\n$3$\n$0$\n$-15$\n$0$\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})({x}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})(x+\\sqrt{5})(x-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{5}{3},-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{3}-\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd39ce5c-84e7-5c84-80a3-e832936bcb9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080901} {:answer/id #uuid "e05064af-8aae-5173-abc4-67fa5a164134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080902} {:answer/id #uuid "6892bc6b-2b5c-5791-b0ca-c9ad0110bb7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080903} {:answer/id #uuid "6b018bba-a62b-546a-b321-cf1b3d7ccfa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080904} {:answer/id #uuid "73cfcd14-dbc0-552c-8551-a80fd9d8470d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd39ce5c-84e7-5c84-80a3-e832936bcb9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080901} :answer-field/id #uuid "533b86c2-12ca-5189-81a9-58ef47774418" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e7bd90fd-0fb1-553e-84d3-ad65f76b3093" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-39038" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x) = x^{3} - 7x^{2} + 18x - 18$ has a root in the interval $(2, 7)$, find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-18$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-18}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9,\\,\\pm 18.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(2,7),$ are $3$ and $6.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$3$\n$1$\n$-7$\n$18$\n$-18$\n\n \n\n$↓$\n$3$\n$-12$\n$18$\n\n \n\n$1$\n$-4$\n$6$\n$0$\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}-4x+6).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)={x}^{2}-4x+6$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-4{)}^{2}-4(1)(6) \\\\  & =16-24 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083807 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3b8c48b-16bd-566a-9423-61494d094e1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082209} {:answer/id #uuid "0f09da8b-cd94-5764-bf77-9bac74a41913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082210} {:answer/id #uuid "108f8a4e-4d9b-5793-9983-e670c1de4601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082211} {:answer/id #uuid "1d7c2dca-37d0-5441-8558-7d14e3faa7d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082212} {:answer/id #uuid "60acd2a7-639f-51ea-8491-eb83e434dc95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d7c2dca-37d0-5441-8558-7d14e3faa7d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082212} :answer-field/id #uuid "12bc9aca-eca8-5388-81cc-4607fc2dee59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3698582-22d9-54f7-afa9-667b63cf2255" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-6676" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x) = 3x^{3} - 12x^{2} - 3x + 12$?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $12$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }12}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{1}{2}$ does not take the above form. The denominator $(2)$ is not a factor of $3.$"}]}
